2.23.m:
Attached [to residual karma], he undoubtedly goes from hell to an animal womb;
even there, O Bhārgava, he does not experience the sin in its entirety.
Then, with marks determined [by his acts], he experiences the remaining [karma] in human form.

Just as a rope bound with many strands has the power to pull heavy things,
so merit for heaven and sin for hell operate here for men, O foremost of the Bhṛgus.
2.114
Puṣkara said:
The soul having entered the womb remains in the embryonic mass;
stupified in that embryonic mass, it remains for just one month.
During the second month, it remains solid;
and the formation of its limbs occurs in the third month.
The epidermis and skin develop in the fifth month; in the sixth, the arising of body hairs occurs;
and likewise in the seventh month, its consciousness awakens.
Even as a frog afflicted by cold becomes dormant in the cold, so that soul,
situated within the womb, remains stupified for six months, O knower of dharma.
In the seventh month, it receives what is drunk and eaten by the mother;
then, in the eighth and ninth months, it suffers intense distress.
Enveloped by the caul, its hands are perpetually folded over its head;
a hermaphrodite lies in the middle, a female on the left, and a male on the right.

the second is blood-bearing, regarded as resting upon the liver and spleen; / the third is fat-bearing, which rests upon subtle and gross parts;
the fourth rests upon the marrow and is regarded as devoted to phlegm; / another is feces-bearing, by its residence in the colon;
the sixth is named bile-bearing, resting upon the digestive fire; / and the seventh is to be known as resting in the seminal receptacle, semen-bearing.
The five sense organs of intellect here are the ear, the nose, the eyes, / the skin, and the tongue; they have their foundation in the gross elements.
Likewise, the objects of the senses are five; hear their names from me: / sound is said to belong to the ear, [and] earthy smell belongs to the breath;
form is to be known of the eye, and touch belongs likewise to the skin; / and taste belongs to the tongue: they indeed have the gross elements as their support.
Here the organs of action are five; hear their names from me: / the anus and the genitals, the hands and the feet, and here the tongue is the fifth.

And from the waste product of blood arises that called the daṇḍaka; / and from the refined essence of fat and blood, the origin of the two kidneys is recorded.
From the refined essence of blood and flesh arise the intestines of embodied beings; / for women, one should designate them as numbering three and a half vyāmas;
people who know the Veda declare three vyāmas for women; / from the union of blood and wind is recorded the origin of the black [peritoneum].
From the refined essence of phlegm arises the heart resembling a lotus, / turned downward and hollow, in which the living being resides.
All the states accompanied by consciousness reside there; / on its left is the spleen, and on its right is the liver;
and on the right of that lotus, the kloman is proclaimed; / the channels that are in this body, carrying phlegm and blood,
from those, following the gross elements, the origin of the organs takes place. / The white circle of the two eyes comes from phlegm; the bilious [one comes from bile];

Sixteen tendons are declared in the body of embodied beings: / two are in each hand, and two are in each foot separately;
four are to be known as going to the back, and just as many in the neck. / Exactly sixteen are the nets here; their division is declared:
four each from flesh, sinews, vessels, and bones separately, / the wrists and ankles bound mutually;
and pegs are recorded here in the hands and in the feet, / and in the neck and the penis, declared by the wise.
And in this body are to be known four ropes of flesh; / two each are proclaimed there running on both sides of the backbone;
and just as many are these that bind them. / And seven are the sutures; five reside in the head,
one situated in the penis, and another situated in the tongue.
Here, the bones are three hundred and sixty,

including the fine ones: sixty-four teeth, twenty nails,
and the bone-pins of the hands and feet, which have four locations;
likewise, forty bones in the two shanks, and just as many [in the arms];
two each arising in the knees, cheeks, lips, shoulder blades, and shoulders;
eight in the eye-sockets; one should likewise assign [two] to the hip bones;
one pubic bone, and forty-five in the back;
fifteen bones of the neck, one collarbone, and likewise the jaw;
two at the root of the eyebrows, the forehead, eye[-sockets], cheeks, and nose-bones;
together with the ribs, sockets, and tumors, seventy-two;
two temples, four skull bones, and likewise the head;
the chest has seventeen bones: this is the enumeration of a person's bones.
And likewise, the joints here are two hundred and one hundred more;
sixty-eight in the limbs, sixty minus one [in the head and neck],
and eighty in the trunk are declared, O descendant of Bhṛgu;

nor are the ligaments considered here to be [only] two hundred, but thirty more;
and seventy ascending [ligaments] are declared in the trunk;
six [hundred] in the limbs have been declared by the wise.
Here there are five hundred muscles, and forty ascending;
four hundred here in the limbs [are taught] by the learned,
and sixty muscles in the trunk are declared by the wise;
and in women, thirty-four more should be known:
ten should be known in the breasts, and likewise ten in the vagina, O Rāma;
likewise four should be known in the uterus by students of gestation.
Thirty hundred thousand, and ten others indeed,
[and] fifty-six thousand vessels are declared;
they carry the nutrient fluid in the body, as channels do to a field,
[absorbing] anointing and everything else, such as sweat and ointments.
Know, O mighty-armed one, that there are seventy-two crores of body hairs;

of marrow, of fat, and of tallow, O twice-born,
of urine, and of bile, phlegm, and feces,
and of blood and nutrient fluid, the handfuls are remembered in order:
each is remembered as half a handful greater than the preceding half-handful;
half a handful of semen, and likewise half of vital essence;
and of the menstrual blood of women, four are declared by the wise.
This measure is stated by students of the body for one of balanced elements.
The bodies of embodied beings are always varied;
they differ according to their differentiation, conforming to wastes and humoral faults.
This body is a lump of wastes and humoral faults, the resting-place of the elements, bound under the sway of karman;
fortunate is he for whom in this world it leads to liberation, and the supreme cause of liberation is indeed Viṣṇu.

2.24.m:
experiencing pain there, he does not die, O giver of honor.
That path is declared to be exceedingly rough and dreadful to behold;
assuming all forms, O Rāma, covered with cold on earth, vast,
abounding in falls of snow, extremely cold, and exceedingly difficult to traverse;

And those who inflict the torments of the hells upon men / are declared to have terrible forms, inspiring terror at mere sight.
Some have the faces of cats, owls, jackals, vultures, hawks, and donkeys; / others, of elephants, horses, camels, boars, and lions;
and of monitor lizards, buffaloes, crows, madgu cormorants, and crane birds; / of rams, goats, moths, serpents, and mice;
and of other creatures too; likewise, others are pot-bellied men; / others have faces on their shoulders, appearing headless like a trunk;
having terrible faces, terrifying, bearing various weapons, / of harsh speech, unassailable, entwined with serpents and scorpions.
Throwing men into a vat of oil, kindling a fire / that is well blazing, they boil some men again and again.
And others on frying pans, and others in copper vessels, / and others in iron vessels, and even in molten sparks of fire;
having impaled others on stakes, and others in dry fire, / cutting them open, they thoroughly cook them, and likewise others after cutting them up.
